  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Sunday)\nIssued at 1202 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nA change in the winds is coming to the area this week. The last\ncouple of days, we have been under the influence of a large upper\nlevel ridge that has been giving us southwest flow in the higher\nparts of the atmosphere. Through tomorrow, an upper level low \nspinning over the pacific northwest will kick eastward. This \nlow will shift upper level winds over the CWA from the southwest \nto west. This means passing storm chances, then slightly cooler \ntemps. And the longer term forecast is hinting at bigger swings in\ntemps that could mean bigger breaks from the heat.\n\nAt the surface, an area of low pressure will develop near north\ncentral Wyoming later today. This low is expected to track \nnortheast tonight through tomorrow. And as it moves east \ntomorrow, north of the CWA, it will swing a cold front through the\narea. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms are likely as these changes happen\nthrough tomorrow. For today, storms are expected to pop up over \nthe Black Hills during the heat of the day and these storms will\nmove east-to-northeast through the afternoon and evening. At the \nsame time, more storms are likely to develop to our south and \nthose will drift northwards later this evening and overnight.\nSevere weather can't be ruled out with hail and strong winds being\nthe main concerns. The SPC has placed a Marginal risk centered\nover the Black Hills and the surrounding plains. Machine learning\nsevere weather models are also highlighting the same area.\n\nLate tonight into early Monday, the previously mentioned cold\nfront will slide west to east over the area. pushing any \nlingering precip, or new rain/storm development, eastward. Based \non current projections though, the bulk of any rain and showers \ntomorrow will be gone by midday with mostly sunny to partly cloudy\nskies Monday afternoon. The rest of the week looks dry with the \nnext round of rain possible next weekend. Once the front pushes \nthrough, westerly winds will pick up some speed. Monday afternoon \nwinds will be strongest in NE WY at upwards of 20 to 25 mph, gusts\nover 30 mph. Then Tuesday, the strongest winds will shift to the \nNW SD plains at around 20 to 30 mph, gusts nearing 40 mph.\n\nLastly, the temperature outlook for this week has some big swings.\nAfternoon highs take a dip after today and tomorrow's highs in \nthe upper 80s to 90s with Tuesday and Wednesday's highs in the 70s\nto 80s. Highs in the 80s and 90s return Thursday and Friday. Next\nweekend could see temps below normal, stay tuned.",
